  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "If \\(yz:zx:xy=1:2:3,\\) then \\(\\dfrac{x}{yz}:\\dfrac{y}{zx}\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(3{:}2\\)",
    "B": "\\(1{:}2\\)",
    "C": "\\(1{:}4\\)",
    "D": "\\(2{:}1\\)",
    "E": "\\(4{:}1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The given ratio has three nonzero terms, so \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\) are all nonzero. We may therefore divide and cancel their factors. Comparing the second product with the first gives\n\\[\n\\frac{zx}{yz}=\\frac21,\n\\qquad \\frac xy=2.\n\\]\nA ratio's value is its first term divided by its second. For the requested ratio, this becomes\n\\[\n\\frac{x/(yz)}{y/(zx)}\n=\\frac{x}{yz}\\cdot\\frac{zx}{y}\n=\\frac{x^2}{y^2}\n=\\left(\\frac xy\\right)^2\n=4.\n\\]\nThus the ratio is \\(4:1\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
